<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salvador</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Salvador is a city on the north-eastern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, founded in 1549 by the first Governor-General, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tome-de-sousa">Tomé de Sousa</a>. It served as the capital of Portuguese colonial Brazil until 1763 and was a major centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> economy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> treat its foundation as the start of a more systematic colonial administration in Brazil.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He founded <b>Salvador</b> on All Saints Bay, divided into an upper administrative town and a lower harbour, and the diocese followed in 1551.</blockquote><a href="/empire/colonial-brazil">Colonial Brazil · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>This decentralised model produced very uneven results and was partly superseded in 1549 by the appointment of a governor-general, Tomé de Sousa, who founded <b>Salvador</b> as the colonial capital and began building a more systematic administration.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In Cuba the result was the religion called Santería or Regla de Ocha, with its own priests, initiations and Ifa diviners; in Brazil, Candomblé, whose first great houses were founded by African priestesses in <b>Salvador</b> in the early 19th century; in Trinidad, the Orisha religion.</blockquote><a href="/religion/yoruba-religion">Yoruba religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>During the unstable regency period in the Empire of Brazil, discontent in <b>Salvador</b> over central control and military recruitment for the war in Rio Grande do Sul came to a head.</blockquote><a href="/state/bahia-republic">Bahia Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The crown bought back the captaincy, and in 1549 Tomé de Sousa founded <b>Salvador</b> there as the capital of the State of Brazil.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-of-bahia">Captaincy of Bahia</a></li><li><blockquote>The captaincy passed through several noble families, including the Dukes of Aveiro, while <b>Salvador</b> to the north overshadowed it.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-of-porto-seguro">Captaincy of Porto Seguro</a></li></ul></section>
